  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the magnitude and phase of $C e^{j\\omega t}$ by reading $|C|$ as the constant amplitude and $\\angle C + \\omega t$ as the time-varying angle that links complex-exponential form to rotating phasors and sinusoidal components.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see $x(t)=C e^{j\\omega t}$, read the expression as a constant complex coefficient multiplying a rotating unit-magnitude exponential. The coefficient $C$ sets the constant magnitude, and the exponential supplies the time-varying angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That base case is the anchor: $e^{j\\omega t}$ already has magnitude $1$ and phase $\\omega t$. So multiplying by $C$ can only scale the radius and add a fixed angle to the rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the coefficient in polar form as $C=|C|e^{j\\angle C}$ so its magnitude and angle are explicit. Then the coefficient and the rotating exponential combine in one read-off rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C=|C|e^{j\\angle C}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad C e^{j\\omega t}=|C|e^{j(\\angle C+\\omega t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude of $C e^{j\\omega t}$ is $|C|$, and the phase is $\\angle C+\\omega t$. That is the rule this topic will reuse: separate the constant amplitude from the time-varying angle, and the same complex exponential becomes a rotating phasor with a clear sinusoidal interpretation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding Magnitude and Phase of a Unit Complex Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the magnitude and phase of $x(t)=e^{j7t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit-coefficient signal $x(t)=e^{j7t}$, so read it as $C e^{j\\omega t}$ with $C=1$ and $\\omega=7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C=1,\\qquad |C|=1,\\qquad \\angle C=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the coefficient is $1$, it contributes no scaling beyond a unit magnitude and no extra angle. The only time-varying part is the rotation $7t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=1,\\qquad \\angle x(t)=7t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude is $1$ and the phase is $7t$. This is the base case that later examples will extend when the coefficient is no longer $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Magnitude and Phase with a Positive Real Coefficient",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the magnitude and phase of $x(t)=4e^{j5t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the positive real coefficient in $x(t)=4e^{j5t}$, so read it as $C e^{j\\omega t}$ with $C=4$ and $\\omega=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C=4,\\qquad |4|=4,\\qquad \\angle 4=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $4$ is positive and real, it contributes only magnitude scaling. The phase comes only from the rotating term $e^{j5t}$, so there is no extra angle from the coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=4,\\qquad \\angle x(t)=5t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude is $4$ and the phase is $5t$. This is the same read-off rule as the unit case, with the coefficient changing only the radius.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding Magnitude and Phase with a Polar Coefficient",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the magnitude and phase of $x(t)=4e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j2t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the polar coefficient in $x(t)=4e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j2t}$. Read it as $C e^{j\\omega t}$ with $C=4e^{j\\pi/6}$ and $\\omega=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C=4e^{j\\pi/6},\\qquad |C|=4,\\qquad \\angle C=\\pi/6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the coefficient is already in polar form, its magnitude is the radius $4$ and its angle is $\\pi/6$. The rotating exponential contributes the additional angle $2t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j2t}=4e^{j(\\pi/6+2t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude is $4$ and the phase is $\\pi/6+2t$. This is the same read-off rule as before, with the coefficient angle carried straight into the total phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding Magnitude and Phase with a Rectangular Coefficient",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the magnitude and phase of $x(t)=(1-j\\sqrt{3})e^{j2t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the rectangular coefficient in $x(t)=(1-j\\sqrt{3})e^{j2t}$. Treat the signal as $C e^{j\\omega t}$ with $C=1-j\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\omega=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|1-j\\sqrt{3}|=\\sqrt{1^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2}=2,\\qquad \\angle(1-j\\sqrt{3})=-\\pi/3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient lies in quadrant IV, so its principal angle is $-\\pi/3$. Rewrite it in polar form as $2e^{-j\\pi/3}$ before combining it with the rotating term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2e^{-j\\pi/3}e^{j2t}=2e^{j(2t-\\pi/3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude is $2$ and the phase is $2t-\\pi/3$. The rectangular coefficient has been converted to polar form, and its angle is carried directly into the total phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Phase at a Specific Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate the phase of $x(t)=2e^{j(\\pi/4+3t)}$ at $t=\\pi/6$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phase expression inside the exponential: $\\pi/4+3t$. The magnitude stays fixed at $2$, so the only job here is to substitute the given time into the phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle x(t)=\\pi/4+3t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $t=\\pi/6$ into the phase formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle x\\left(\\pi/6\\right)=\\pi/4+3\\left(\\pi/6\\right)=\\pi/4+\\pi/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify the angle to a single exact value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle x\\left(\\pi/6\\right)=3\\pi/4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase at $t=\\pi/6$ is $3\\pi/4$. The magnitude remains $2$, but the phase evaluation is the new step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
